"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[22418],{222418:e=>{e.exports="# SELECT, DML, and Transactions\n\nSnowflake SELECT patterns, window functions, DML, recursive CTEs, MERGE, and transaction handling → DBSQL.\n\n> Semi-structured access (`:` paths, `OBJECT_CONSTRUCT`, `ARRAY_CONSTRUCT`, `FLATTEN`) lives in `references/snowflake/03-semi-structured.md`.\n> For DBSQL MERGE syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/data-modification.md`.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### SELECT patterns\n\n| Snowflake                                                                         | DBSQL                                   | Notes                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SELECT * FROM t LIMIT 10`                                                        | `SELECT * FROM t LIMIT 10`              | Same                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `SELECT TOP 10 * FROM t ORDER BY col`                                             | `SELECT * FROM t ORDER BY col LIMIT 10` | Snowflake TOP without ORDER BY is legal; DBSQL LIMIT behaves the same                                                                                                         |\n| `SELECT ... SAMPLE (10 ROWS)`                                                     | `SELECT ... TABLESAMPLE (10 ROWS)`      |                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `SELECT ... SAMPLE (10)` — **percent by default** (BERNOULLI, probability sample) | `SELECT ... TABLESAMPLE (10 PERCENT)`   | **Bare `SAMPLE(N)` is percentage-based, not row-count.** Mapping it to `(10 ROWS)` silently shrinks the sample from ~10% to 10 rows.                                          |\n| `SELECT ... SAMPLE (10 PERCENT)`                                                  | `SELECT ... TABLESAMPLE (10 PERCENT)`   |                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `SELECT ... SAMPLE BERNOULLI (10)`                                                | `TABLESAMPLE (10 PERCENT)`              | Bernoulli is row-level sampling — same meaning                                                                                                                                |\n| `SELECT ... SAMPLE SYSTEM (10)`                                                   | No direct block-sampling equivalent     | Use `TABLESAMPLE (N PERCENT)` (Spark has no block-sampling unit)                                                                                                              |\n| `QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...) = 1`                                             | `QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...) = 1`   | **Supported identically** (DBR 10.4 LTS+)                                                                                                                                     |\n| `PIVOT (SUM(x) FOR col IN ('Q1','Q2'))`                                           | `PIVOT (SUM(x) FOR col IN ('Q1','Q2'))` | Both support; same syntax                                                                                                                                                     |\n| `UNPIVOT (val FOR name IN (c1, c2))`                                              | `UNPIVOT (val FOR name IN (c1, c2))`    | Supported in recent DBR and Serverless                                                                                                                                        |\n| `GROUP BY ALL`                                                                    | `GROUP BY ALL`                          | DBR 12.2 LTS+                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `SELECT * EXCLUDE (col1, col2)`                                                   | `SELECT * EXCEPT (col1, col2)`          | Syntax differs                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `SELECT * RENAME (old AS new)`                                                    | No direct equivalent                    | Use explicit column list or subquery                                                                                                                                          |\n| `MATCH_RECOGNIZE(...)`                                                            | **No DBSQL equivalent — TODO**          | Row-pattern matching; manual rewrite with window functions for simple cases                                                                                                   |\n| `(a, b) IS DISTINCT FROM (c, d)` (tuple / row-comparison form)                    | **No DBSQL equivalent — decompose**     | DBSQL `IS DISTINCT FROM` operates on scalar values only. Rewrite: `a IS DISTINCT FROM c OR b IS DISTINCT FROM d` (tuple form is true when ANY position differs — OR, not AND) |\n| `(a, b) IS NOT DISTINCT FROM (c, d)` (tuple / row-comparison form)                | **No DBSQL equivalent — decompose**     | Rewrite: `a IS NOT DISTINCT FROM c AND b IS NOT DISTINCT FROM d`                                                                                                              |\n\n### LISTAGG (string aggregation)\n\nSee `references/snowflake/00-quirks.md` for the full example. Summary:\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nLISTAGG(name, ',') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY priority DESC)\n\n-- DBSQL option 1 (preferred — LISTAGG with WITHIN GROUP is a direct substitution, DBR 16.4+)\nLISTAGG(name, ',') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY priority DESC)\n\n-- DBSQL option 2 (pre-16.4 DBR — ARRAY_SORT approach)\nARRAY_JOIN(\n  TRANSFORM(\n    ARRAY_SORT(COLLECT_LIST(STRUCT(priority, name)),\n               (a,b) -> CASE WHEN a.priority > b.priority THEN -1\n                             WHEN a.priority < b.priority THEN 1 ELSE 0 END),\n    x -> x.name\n  ),\n  ','\n)\n\n-- DBSQL (unordered — when order does not matter)\nARRAY_JOIN(COLLECT_LIST(name), ',')\n-- Or: STRING_AGG(name, ',') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY priority DESC)  -- ordered form also available (DBR 16.4+)\n```\n\n### Recursive CTEs\n\nBoth dialects support standard and recursive CTEs. Two differences to watch:\n\n- Snowflake: `RECURSIVE` keyword is optional.\n- DBSQL: `RECURSIVE` keyword is **required**, and recursive CTEs are supported only on **DBR 17.0+**. On earlier DBR versions, rewrite with iterative scripting or pre-computed depth.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nWITH employee_tree AS (\n  SELECT id, name, manager_id, 1 AS lvl FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id, t.lvl + 1\n  FROM employees e JOIN employee_tree t ON e.manager_id = t.id\n)\nSELECT * FROM employee_tree;\n\n-- DBSQL (add RECURSIVE, use UC three-part names)\nWITH RECURSIVE employee_tree AS (\n  SELECT id, name, manager_id, 1 AS lvl\n  FROM my_catalog.my_schema.employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id, t.lvl + 1\n  FROM my_catalog.my_schema.employees e JOIN employee_tree t ON e.manager_id = t.id\n)\nSELECT * FROM employee_tree;\n```\n\n> For DBSQL recursive CTE syntax and depth limits, load `skills/writing-sql/recursive-ctes.md`.\n\n### Cursors (anti-pattern)\n\n**Cursors are an anti-pattern in Databricks.** Databricks is optimized for parallel, set-based operations. Row-by-row processing eliminates parallelism benefits.\n\nRewrite as set-based operations wherever possible:\n\n```sql\n-- Instead of a cursor updating row-by-row:\nUPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.other_table SET processed = 1\nWHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source_table);\n\n-- Running totals — window function instead of cursor:\nSELECT date, amount,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY date) AS running_total\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.transactions;\n```\n\n> For literal cursor translation when a set-based rewrite is not feasible, see `references/snowflake/05b-scripting-control-flow.md`. Cursor syntax is supported in Databricks Serverless SQL via SQL scripting.\n\n### String operations\n\n| Snowflake                                     | DBSQL                                              | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `a \\|\\| b`                                    | `a \\|\\| b` or `CONCAT(a, b)`                       | Both propagate NULL                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `CONCAT(a, b, ...)`                           | `CONCAT(a, b, ...)`                                | Both propagate NULL                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `CONCAT_WS(sep, a, b, ...)`                   | `CONCAT_WS(sep, a, b, ...)`                        | **Silent NULL drift** — Snowflake propagates NULL; DBSQL skips NULLs. See `references/snowflake/00-quirks.md`                                                                                                       |\n| `SPLIT(str, delim)`                           | `SPLIT(str, delim)`                                | **Drift — delimiter is regex in DBSQL**; escape metachars (see `00-quirks.md`)                                                                                                                                      |\n| `SPLIT_PART(str, delim, n)`                   | `SPLIT_PART(str, delim, n)`                        | DBR 10.4+; same semantics                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `CHARINDEX(sub, str)`                         | `CHARINDEX(sub, str)`                              | Both support                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `POSITION(sub IN str)`                        | `POSITION(sub IN str)` or `POSITION(sub, str)`     | DBSQL supports both forms — no rewrite needed. **Do NOT rewrite to `LOCATE`** as `locate(str, sub)` — both `POSITION` and `LOCATE` take `(substr, str)` order; reversing arguments silently produces wrong indices. |\n| `REGEXP_LIKE(str, pattern)`                   | `str RLIKE pattern` or `REGEXP_LIKE(str, pattern)` | Both supported in DBSQL                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `REGEXP_REPLACE(str, pat, rep)`               | `REGEXP_REPLACE(str, pat, rep)`                    | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `REGEXP_SUBSTR(str, pat)`                     | `REGEXP_EXTRACT(str, pat, 0)`                      | **Default idx differs** — Snowflake default returns the whole match; DBSQL default idx is 1 (first group). Pass `0` explicitly.                                                                                     |\n| `REGEXP_COUNT(str, pat)`                      | `SIZE(SPLIT(str, pat)) - 1`                        | No direct equivalent                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `REGEXP_INSTR(str, pat)`                      | No direct equivalent                               | Use `LOCATE` with regex helper or a Python UDF                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `CONTAINS(str, sub)`                          | `CONTAINS(str, sub)`                               | Supported                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `STARTSWITH(str, pre)` / `ENDSWITH(str, suf)` | `STARTSWITH(str, pre)` / `ENDSWITH(str, suf)`      | Supported                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `INITCAP(str)`                                | `INITCAP(str)`                                     | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `RPAD(str, n, fill)` / `LPAD(str, n, fill)`   | `RPAD(str, n, fill)` / `LPAD(str, n, fill)`        | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n\n### DML patterns\n\n| Snowflake                                           | DBSQL                                                      | Notes                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `INSERT INTO ... SELECT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS`       | Same                                                       |                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `UPDATE t SET col = s.col FROM s WHERE t.id = s.id` | `MERGE INTO t USING s ...`                                 | DBSQL Delta **does not support `UPDATE ... FROM`**; MERGE is the documented migration target. Correlated subqueries in `SET` are not reliably supported on Delta — avoid. |\n| `DELETE FROM t USING s WHERE t.id = s.id`           | `DELETE FROM t WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM s)`             | DBSQL has no `USING` clause in DELETE                                                                                                                                     |\n| `MERGE INTO t USING s ON ...` multi-action          | Same — MERGE multi-action supported                        |                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `TRUNCATE TABLE t`                                  | `TRUNCATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t`                    | Same                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `CREATE TABLE t AS SELECT ...`                      | `CREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t AS SELECT ...`        | Same                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `INSERT OVERWRITE INTO t SELECT ...`                | `INSERT OVERWRITE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t SELECT ...` | Both supported                                                                                                                                                            |\n\n**UPDATE ... FROM — translate to MERGE:**\n\nDBSQL Delta does not support the `UPDATE ... FROM` syntax. The documented migration target is MERGE.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nUPDATE orders t\nSET t.status = s.new_status\nFROM order_updates s\nWHERE t.order_id = s.order_id;\n\n-- DBSQL: MERGE\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.order_updates s ON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET status = s.new_status;\n```\n\n### MERGE — multi-action (supported identically)\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nMERGE INTO target t\nUSING source s ON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED AND s.deleted = TRUE THEN DELETE\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.val = s.val, t.updated_at = s.updated_at\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (id, val, updated_at) VALUES (s.id, s.val, s.updated_at);\n\n-- DBSQL (same syntax, UC three-part names; both qualified and unqualified SET work)\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.target t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.source s ON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED AND s.deleted = TRUE THEN DELETE\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.val = s.val, t.updated_at = s.updated_at\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (id, val, updated_at) VALUES (s.id, s.val, s.updated_at);\n```\n\n**Differences:**\n\n- `WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE` (ANSI standard) is supported in DBSQL (DBR 12.2 LTS+) but **not** in Snowflake. If the migration target needs delete-by-absence semantics, this is a DBSQL-only addition — there is nothing to translate from.\n- Both dialects evaluate `WHEN MATCHED` conditions top-down and stop at the first match.\n- Both use optimistic concurrency with conflict detection. Delta MERGE may throw a concurrent-modification exception under contention; handle with retry.\n- SET clause: both dialects accept qualified (`t.col = ...`) and unqualified (`col = ...`) column names. No rewrite needed.\n\n**Empty source (`SELECT … WHERE 1 = 0`) requires `FROM`/`VALUES` in DBSQL.** Snowflake permits a bare `SELECT 1, 'x' WHERE 1 = 0` (no FROM clause) — typically used inside `MERGE INTO target USING (SELECT … WHERE 1 = 0) AS src ON …` to install a no-op MERGE. DBSQL's parser **requires** a `FROM` (or `FROM (VALUES …) AS t(...)`) — a bare `SELECT … WHERE` with no FROM raises a parse error.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake (legal — empty source for a no-op MERGE)\nMERGE INTO target t\nUSING (\n  SELECT current_date AS d1, 'x'::VARCHAR AS event_name WHERE 1 = 0\n) AS src ON t.d1 = src.d1\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (d1, event_name) VALUES (src.d1, src.event_name);\n\n-- DBSQL — use VALUES to materialise an empty rowset of the right shape\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.target t\nUSING (\n  SELECT *\n  FROM (VALUES (CAST(NULL AS DATE), CAST(NULL AS STRING))) AS v(d1, event_name)\n  WHERE 1 = 0\n) AS src ON t.d1 = src.d1\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (d1, event_name) VALUES (src.d1, src.event_name);\n```\n\nThe `VALUES` row gets filtered out by `WHERE 1 = 0`, but the SELECT now has a FROM and parses. Apply the same `VALUES`-with-WHERE pattern any time the Snowflake source has a top-level `SELECT … WHERE 1 = 0` without FROM.\n\n### OUTPUT / RETURNING equivalents\n\nSnowflake does not have an `OUTPUT` clause on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. Common Snowflake pattern for capturing affected rows is a MERGE followed by a SELECT over the source, or using `RESULT_SCAN(LAST_QUERY_ID())` to re-read the last query's rows.\n\nDBSQL also has no direct `OUTPUT` clause. Pattern:\n\n```sql\n-- Capture merge activity by joining target to source after the MERGE\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.target t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.source s ON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET val = s.val\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (id, val) VALUES (s.id, s.val);\n\n-- After the merge, record what was changed\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.audit_log\nSELECT 'MERGE', t.id\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.target t\nINNER JOIN my_catalog.my_schema.source s ON t.id = s.id;\n-- Note: captures post-merge state; does not distinguish insert vs update\n-- without additional bookkeeping (e.g. a pre-merge snapshot).\n```\n\nFor row-level change tracking, enable Change Data Feed (`delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true`) and query `table_changes()` after the merge (see `references/snowflake/07-pipelines.md` STREAM section).\n\n### Transactions\n\nSnowflake supports multi-statement transactions with `BEGIN` / `COMMIT` / `ROLLBACK`. Autocommit is on by default; explicit `BEGIN` starts a transaction.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nBEGIN;\n  INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n  INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL (interactive multi-statement)\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t1 VALUES (1);\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t2 VALUES (2);\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL (recommended: ATOMIC block with auto-rollback on error)\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t1 VALUES (1);\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n```\n\n**Critical limitations:**\n\n1. **No nested transactions** in DBSQL — Snowflake's inner `BEGIN` is also a no-op (not true nesting). If the source relies on nested-transaction logic, flag for rewrite.\n2. **No savepoints** — neither dialect supports `SAVEPOINT`. If the source is from a different dialect (e.g., PostgreSQL) passing through Snowflake, handle at that layer.\n3. **No DDL inside transactions in DBSQL** — Snowflake allows `CREATE TABLE` inside a transaction. Move all DDL outside the `BEGIN TRANSACTION` / `COMMIT` block.\n\n**Runtime note:** `BEGIN ATOMIC` works on SQL warehouses, serverless compute, and DBR 17.0+ for single-table atomic blocks. Multi-table atomic transactions require DBR 18.0+. On earlier runtimes, use `BEGIN TRANSACTION` / `COMMIT`.\n\n### Nested transactions (unsupported in both, but flagged differently)\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake (inner BEGIN is silently accepted; effectively no-op)\nBEGIN;\n  INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n  BEGIN;          -- Snowflake ignores; single logical transaction\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\n  COMMIT;         -- this does NOT commit anything on its own in Snowflake\nCOMMIT;           -- final commit\n\n-- DBSQL: flatten to a single ATOMIC block\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t1 VALUES (1);\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n```\n\n### Savepoints\n\nNeither Snowflake nor DBSQL supports `SAVEPOINT` / `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT`. No translation is needed for Snowflake → DBSQL. If `SAVEPOINT` appears in the source, it was likely pasted from a different dialect (e.g., PostgreSQL or Oracle) and already did not execute on Snowflake — rewrite the surrounding logic into discrete atomic steps.\n\n### FOR UPDATE / locking hints\n\nSnowflake supports `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` only on **hybrid (Unistore) tables** — standard Snowflake tables are snapshot-based and ignore `FOR UPDATE`. DBSQL Delta is MVCC and has no row-level lock syntax at all.\n\nIf `FOR UPDATE` appears in the source:\n\n- **On standard Snowflake tables**: it was a no-op; remove it during migration.\n- **On hybrid tables**: the locking semantics do not carry over — flag for application-level rewrite (optimistic concurrency with retry, or Delta MERGE with conflict detection).\n\n### Transaction isolation\n\nSnowflake: `READ COMMITTED`, snapshot per statement. DBSQL Delta: snapshot isolation per transaction.\n\n`SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...` — not needed in DBSQL; remove.\n\n### DDL inside transactions (DBSQL blocks this)\n\nDBSQL cannot run `CREATE`, `ALTER`, or `DROP` inside a `BEGIN TRANSACTION` / `COMMIT` block. Snowflake allows it. Move DDL outside the transaction.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake (works — DDL inside transaction)\nBEGIN;\n  CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE staging LIKE my_table;\n  INSERT INTO staging SELECT * FROM source;\n  MERGE INTO my_table USING staging ON ...;\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL: create the staging view/table BEFORE the transaction\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW staging AS SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source;\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n  MERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.my_table USING staging ON ...;\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\n### Safe functions (no translation needed)\n\n`SUBSTR` / `SUBSTRING`, `LEFT`, `RIGHT`, `TRIM`, `LTRIM`, `RTRIM`, `REPLACE`, `REVERSE`, `UPPER`, `LOWER`, `INITCAP`, `ABS`, `FLOOR`, `CEIL` / `CEILING`, `ROUND`, `SQRT`, `POWER`, `MOD`, `SIGN`, `COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `UNION`, `EXCEPT`, `INTERSECT`, `RANK`, `DENSE_RANK`, `ROW_NUMBER`, `LEAD`, `LAG`, `FIRST_VALUE`, `LAST_VALUE`, `NTILE`, `PERCENT_RANK`, `CUME_DIST`.\n\n**Note — `MINUS` set operator**: Both dialects accept `MINUS` and `EXCEPT` as synonyms — no rewrite needed.\n\n### System catalog and metadata queries\n\n| Snowflake                                         | DBSQL equivalent                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SHOW TABLES` / `SHOW TABLES IN SCHEMA db.schema` | `SHOW TABLES IN catalog.schema`                                                          |\n| `SHOW COLUMNS IN TABLE db.schema.t`               | `DESCRIBE TABLE catalog.schema.t`                                                        |\n| `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES`                       | `system.information_schema.tables`                                                       |\n| `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS`                      | `system.information_schema.columns`                                                      |\n| `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.VIEWS`                        | `system.information_schema.views`                                                        |\n| `SHOW PARAMETERS`                                 | No equivalent — session config via `SET` / `SHOW` in DBSQL                               |\n| `LAST_QUERY_ID()`                                 | No equivalent — DBSQL has no user-facing query id at SQL level                           |\n| `RESULT_SCAN(query_id)`                           | **No equivalent — TODO**; re-run the query                                               |\n| `CURRENT_VERSION()`                               | `CURRENT_VERSION()` or `VERSION()`                                                       |\n| `CURRENT_DATABASE()`                              | `CURRENT_CATALOG()` — semantic shift: UC catalog, not Snowflake \"database\"               |\n| `CURRENT_SCHEMA()`                                | `CURRENT_SCHEMA()`                                                                       |\n| `CURRENT_WAREHOUSE()`                             | No equivalent; DBSQL uses a different compute concept                                    |\n| `CURRENT_ROLE()`                                  | `CURRENT_USER()` or `IS_MEMBER('group')` — role is not 1-to-1                            |\n| `CURRENT_USER()`                                  | `CURRENT_USER()`                                                                         |\n| `CURRENT_SESSION()`                               | No equivalent                                                                            |\n| `SYSTEM$*` admin functions                        | Most have no DBSQL equivalent — see `references/snowflake/05b-scripting-control-flow.md` |\n\nIf the source uses system catalog queries to enumerate objects and build dynamic SQL, consider replacing with a fixed-schema approach or a Python notebook cell using the Databricks SDK.\n"}}]);
